      'This page introduces the articles filed under operating model. An operating model says who does the work, in what order, with which tools and under whose review. An article in this category describes one routine of that model, such as how a request arrives, who accepts it and how it is closed.',
      'Write each routine as a short list of steps, each with one owner. If a step has two owners, nobody owns it, so pick one and name the other as a reviewer. Give every step an input and an output, because a step with no output cannot be checked and will quietly stop happening.',
      'Describe the handover points with the most care. Work is lost where it passes from one person to another, so say what the sender must provide, what the receiver checks and how long each side has to act. A written handover costs a few minutes and saves the argument later.',
